@startuml
!include ../themes/dark-theme.puml
title Request Guardrails – Multi-Key Rate Limiting

legend Top
Because those three limiters are protecting three different abuse
patterns, and they need different thresholds and time windows. One size won’t fit all.

IP limiter = stops floods and bot storms
Identifier limiter = stops account brute force (targeting one user)
Combo limiter (IP+identifier) = stops a single attacker hammering one account from one IP

If you only do one of these, attackers just switch strategy.
end legend

start
:Receive login request;

:Derive rate limit keys;
note right
  Keys:
  - login:ip:{ip}
  - login:id:{identifier}
  - login:ip+id:{ip}:{identifier}
end note

:Check IP limiter (60 / 1m); <<warning>>
if (IP limited?) then (yes)
  :Return RATE_LIMITED; <<failure>>
  note right
    Source- ipLimiter : 60 requests / 1 minute
    Message from RateLimitResult.Blocked
  end note
  end
endif

:Check Identifier limiter (10 / 10m); <<warning>>
if (Identifier limited?) then (yes)
  :Return RATE_LIMITED; <<failure>>
  note right
    Source - idLimiter : 10 attempts / 10 minutes
    Message from RateLimitResult.Blocked
  end note
  end
endif

:Check IP+Identifier limiter (5 / 5m); <<warning>>
if (Combo limited?) then (yes)
  :Return RATE_LIMITED; <<failure>>
  note right
    Source - comboLimiter : 5 attempts / 5 minutes
    Message from RateLimitResult.Blocked
  end note
  end
endif

:Continue to credential verification;

end
@enduml
